 ## Overview
-Marching Cubes with QEF Vertex Optimization is a surface extraction method that computes vertex positions by minimizing a Quadric Error Function (QEF) for ambiguous topological cases, while employing a fast 2D linear/binomial search for non-ambiguous cases, to produce a watertight mesh from a voxel grid [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard Marching Cubes by replacing the linear interpolation of vertex positions with geometric optimization that respects the local surface curvature defined by the voxel neighborhood, and distinguishes itself from other post-processing methods by integrating this optimization directly into the vertex generation phase rather than as a separate mesh simplification step [S1, unofficial].
